  <section class="lp-section" id="dialer-reviews"><div class="lp-wrap"><div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">03 / What we would actually weigh</p><h2>Ten dialler reviews.<br>The good. The catch. Our call.</h2><p>A clear price deserves credit. An unclear price deserves a question. Neither proves what the prospect will hear when the call connects.</p></div><div class="lp-provider-nav" aria-label="Jump to a dialler">{% for p in guide.providers %}<a href="#{{ p.id }}">{{ p.name }}</a>{% endfor %}</div>
    {% for p in guide.providers %}<article class="lp-provider" id="{{ p.id }}" aria-labelledby="{{ p.id }}-title"><div class="lp-provider-head"><div><p class="lp-kicker">{{ p.tag }}</p><h3 id="{{ p.id }}-title">{{ p.name }}</h3></div><a class="lp-source" href="{{ p.sources[0].url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Check current pricing ↗</a></div><p class="dc-price-strip"><strong>Price check:</strong> {{ p.price }}</p><div class="lp-provider-body"><div class="lp-provider-detail"><h4>What it does well</h4><p>{{ p.strength }}</p><h4>What we do not like</h4><p>{{ p.drawback }}</p></div><aside class="lp-verdict"><p class="lp-kicker">CallTeam verdict</p><p class="lp-verdict-text">{{ p.verdict }}</p><div><strong>What your trial still needs to establish</strong><p>{{ p.check }}</p></div></aside></div><p class="lp-sources"><strong>Official sources:</strong> {% for s in p.sources %}<a href="{{ s.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ s.label }} ↗</a>{% if not loop.last %}<span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p></article>{% endfor %}
    <p class="lp-small">Choosing the CRM as well? Compare <a href="/resources/best-crm-for-outbound-sales/">seven CRMs for outbound sales and cold calling</a>, including subscription costs and callback checks.</p>
    <div class="lp-matchups"><div><h3>Nooks vs Orum</h3><p>Start with the team purchase: Nooks’ own guide says two seats; Orum’s public pricing says nine. Then compare the full coaching package, CRM writeback and simultaneous-answer handling. Use comparable calling blocks. A faster-looking demo is not a controlled answer-rate test.</p></div><div><h3>Close vs Apollo</h3><p>Close is the CRM decision as well as the calling decision. Apollo joins data and outreach, with Advanced Dialer added at team level. If one is already in your workflow, trial that route first. Include migration work and credit charges in the comparison.</p><p>Missing phone data? Review <a href="/resources/apollo-alternatives/">Apollo alternatives for cold calling</a> before replacing the dialler.</p></div><div><h3>PhoneBurner vs Mojo</h3><p>Compare the connection method and callback route you need. PhoneBurner has tiered single-line plans; Mojo separates access from single- or triple-line licences. Add voice, recording and any CRM connector costs before comparing totals.</p></div><div><h3>Aircall vs JustCall vs CloudTalk</h3><p>These deserve attention when the business phone system matters alongside prospecting. Compare the plan that includes your dialling mode, minimum seats, number allowances and exact CRM integration. The cheapest phone plan may not include your sales workflow.</p></div></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="dialer-types"><div class="lp-wrap"><div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">04 / Understand the mode</p><h2>Auto, power, parallel<br>or predictive dialler?</h2><p>“Auto dialler” is a broad label. Ask how the software places calls and assigns a live answer. Product names are not a consistent technical standard.</p></div><div class="dc-modes"><div><h3>Power dialler</h3><p>Usually moves through a queue one number at a time for the rep. A useful place to start when account context, switchboards and careful follow-up matter. Check whether the next call starts automatically and how much control you retain.</p></div><div><h3>Parallel dialler</h3><p>Calls several numbers for a rep and connects a detected answer. Useful when ringing and unanswered calls dominate a clean queue. Test simultaneous answers, detection delay and the queue treatment of cancelled calls.</p></div><div><h3>Predictive dialler</h3><p>Adjusts dialling around expected answers and available agents. Its value depends on the actual team workflow. Ask the vendor to demonstrate what happens when a person answers and no rep is free.</p></div><div><h3>AI dialler or AI voice agent?</h3><p>Answer detection, summaries and coaching help a human caller. A voice agent conducts the conversation itself. This guide covers human calling tools. Assess autonomous agents separately, including their permitted use, escalation and handling of unusual replies.</p></div></div>
    <div class="lp-field-note"><p class="lp-kicker">What we care about on a real call</p><h3>Voicemail and AI screening are different outcomes.</h3><p>A screening assistant may ask who is calling before the person decides whether to answer. Test that behaviour separately from an ordinary voicemail greeting. A connected call is not automatically a conversation with your buyer.</p><p>For company numbers, test a switchboard, extension and shared reception line. For mobiles, test screening, voicemail and callbacks. Do not let several records with the same company number create a burst of calls to one receptionist.</p><p>Our view: keep researched accounts and arranged callbacks in a queue where the caller has time to think. Speed up the repeatable parts once the basics work.</p><a class="lp-text-link" href="/resources/how-to-buy-cold-calling-leads/#number-types">Read the mobile vs company number guide →</a></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section lp-dark" id="trial-scorecard"><div class="lp-wrap"><div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">05 / Free. No email required.</p><h2>Run a trial you<br>can actually compare.</h2><p>Use this kit when you shortlist a vendor, renew a contract or change your calling setup. Keep a dated copy of the results. You will have something better than a memory of the demo.</p></div><ol class="lp-test-steps"><li><strong>Bring a representative queue.</strong><p>Use similar buyer roles, number types and calling windows for each trial. Avoid repeatedly calling the same people just to test software. Record unique contacts separately from attempts.</p></li><li><strong>Test the awkward calls.</strong><p>Check screening assistants, voicemail, switchboards, two simultaneous answers, wrong numbers and stop requests. Test callbacks from a number you control.</p></li><li><strong>Follow the record into the CRM.</strong><p>Check contact matching, notes, dispositions, recording links and next actions. Duplicate activities and missing callbacks create work after the dialling stops.</p></li><li><strong>Compare quality and the invoice.</strong><p>Track useful buyer conversations, technical failures and actual charges. Keep held meetings separate. A short trial can reveal problems; it cannot prove future conversion rates.</p></li></ol>
    <div class="lp-download-row"><div><h3>Your reusable dialler trial kit.</h3><p>A plain-text scorecard and vendor questions. Open it in any notes app, fill it in and keep the results. No account, email gate or prospect data required.</p></div><a class="lp-button" href="/assets/downloads/cold-calling-dialer-trial-kit.txt" download="CallTeam-Dialer-Trial-Kit.txt">Download the free trial kit ↓</a></div>
    <details class="dc-score"><summary>Open and copy the trial scorecard</summary><div class="dc-copy-text" id="dc-scorecard-copy">{% include "components/dialer-scorecard.njk" %}</div><button type="button" class="lp-button dc-js-only" hidden data-dc-copy="dc-scorecard-copy" data-dc-status="dc-scorecard-status">Copy the scorecard</button><span class="dc-copy-status" id="dc-scorecard-status" role="status"></span></details>
    <details class="dc-score"><summary>Open and copy the questions to ask sales</summary><div class="dc-copy-text" id="dc-questions-copy"><p>1. Quote the exact dialler-enabled plan, currency, minimum paid seats, billing term and payment schedule. What renews automatically, and when must we cancel?</p><p>2. Which features are included: power, parallel, predictive, human detection, coaching, recording and callbacks? Which require an add-on or different tier?</p><p>3. What costs extra for numbers, destinations, minutes, credits, voicemail connections and inbound calls? What is the rounding rule? What does “unlimited” exclude?</p><p>4. What happens when two people answer at once, a screening assistant answers, or no rep is free? Show us the call log and retry behaviour.</p><p>5. Show our exact CRM fields, dispositions, duplicate handling and callback tasks. What requires another connector or subscription?</p><p>6. Can we reduce seats mid-term? What happens to our numbers, recordings, notes and unused credits when we leave? Put the answers in the offer.</p></div><button type="button" class="lp-button dc-js-only" hidden data-dc-copy="dc-questions-copy" data-dc-status="dc-questions-status">Copy the vendor questions</button><span class="dc-copy-status" id="dc-questions-status" role="status"></span></details>
    <p class="lp-small">Compare the subscription estimate with the written offer. Check usage and extra charges separately, and keep the offer alongside your trial notes.</p>
    <div class="gr-resources" aria-labelledby="dc-script-resources"><h3 id="dc-script-resources">Bring a prepared script to the trial.</h3><p>Keep the offer and opening consistent across comparable calling sessions. Changing the message and dialler together makes the results harder to interpret.</p>{% include "components/free-calling-resources.njk" %}</div>
  </div></section>

